<description>
Recolor the small defect bars on each shared-color object with the opposite panel color.

Input structure
- The grid contains two large panel colors and one shared object color.
- Each shared-color object is a mostly solid rectangle inside exactly one panel.
Rule
- Find the near-solid rectangular core of each object.
- Missing core pixels and one-cell protrusions define two-pixel bars perpendicular to the nearest core edge.
- Recolor those bars with the other panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged.
Key insight
- The colors to add are not tied to numeric ids: each object's surrounding panel identifies the opposite panel color.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "shared-color near-rectangular objects embedded in exactly one of two panel colors",
  "transform": "for each object, find its near-solid rectangular core; recolor two-pixel bars from missing core-edge pixels or one-cell protrusions to the opposite panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 20-27 rows x 20-27 cols, roughly square [observed]
  colors: 4 distinct colors -- object_color, panel_A, panel_B, background
  layout: |
    Two rectangular panels (panel_A, panel_B) and a background region partition the grid.
    One panel is a pure rectangle; the other is a rectangle or L-shape (rectangle with
    one corner cut where the other panel occupies). Background fills the complement.
    Panels are 4-adjacent (share a border segment, not just a corner).
    Each panel is at least 9x10 cells. Background spans most of the grid perimeter.
  objects:
    color: single shared object_color across all components
    count: 2-4 per grid; 1-2 per panel
    shape: near-solid rectangle (core_rect) with 1-3 single-cell defects
    core_rect: 3-11 wide, 3-9 tall (min dimension >= 3)
    defects: |
      Each defect is one of:
        - missing: 1 cell on exactly one edge of core_rect (not a corner), panel_color fills it
        - protrusion: 1 cell extending exactly 1 cell beyond one face of core_rect, object_color
      Defects per component: 1-3. Each defect has an unambiguous nearest side.
    placement: fully inside its panel; min gap >= 1 cell from panel border (including defect bars)
    separation: object bounding boxes within same panel are non-overlapping with gap >= 2
  transform: |
    For each object, find the core_rect. Each defect generates a 2-cell bar perpendicular
    to the nearest core edge, bridging the boundary. Recolor the bar with the OTHER panel's color
    (the panel that does NOT surround this object). Input pixels outside bars are unchanged.
generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    - Pick 4 distinct colors for object, panel_A, panel_B, background.
    - Choose grid size 20-27 square. Place two panel rectangles that share a border
      segment, with background filling the rest. One panel may be L-shaped.
    - For each panel, decide 1-2 object components (total 2-4).
    - For each object, build its OUTPUT state first -- a solid core_rect (3-9 x 3-11)
      with 2-cell colored bars on 1-3 distinct edges, painted in the opposite panel color.
      Ensure bars stay inside the panel with >= 1 cell margin.
    - Derive the INPUT by undoing the bars -- for each bar, one cell becomes a defect
      (missing pixel reverts to panel_color, protrusion reverts to object_color) and the
      other cell reverts to its original color (panel or object).
    - Verify solver constraints -- each defect has a unique nearest side, core_rect is
      unambiguous, and all object components are 4-connected with >= 5 cells.
```
